 "notes": [
  "After *have*, *has* or *had*, and in every passive, use the third column: *I have **written** the draft*, *the bridge was **built** in 1998*. *I have **wrote*** and *it was **build*** are the two most common slips in student essays.",
  "*be* is the only verb with two past forms: *was* after *I, he, she, it* and *were* after *you, we, they*.",
  "*get*: British writing uses *got* as the past participle (*I have got a new laptop*). *Gotten* is North American and is best avoided in an IELTS answer.",
  "Two different verbs look alike. *lie – lay – lain* means to rest and takes no object (*she lay on the sofa*). *lay – laid – laid* means to put something down and always takes one (*he laid the books on the desk*). The verb *lie* meaning \"not tell the truth\" is regular: *lie – lied – lied*.",
  "*hang* is *hung* for objects (*we hung the poster*), but *hanged* only when it refers to an execution.",
  "*read* is spelt the same in all three columns, but the past forms are pronounced like *red*.",
  "Pairs such as *burnt / burned*, *learnt / learned*, *dreamt / dreamed*, *spelt / spelled* and *spilt / spilled* are both correct. The -t spelling is more usual in British writing; choose one and stay consistent in a piece of work.",
  "A few verbs have an irregular past but a regular partner with a different meaning: *found* is the past of *find*, while *founded* is the past of *found* (to set up an organisation)."
 ]
}
